      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I'll read both the original test and the CUT source to understand the code before improving it.Now I have a clear picture of the original test. The main understandability issues are:\n\n1. The provider method has all 27 `Arguments.of(...)` calls crammed onto one line — nearly unreadable.\n2. The `if` branch with the sentinel-value guard is empty, making the control flow confusing.\n3. The variable name `ntfsTime2` is opaque.\n\nI'll write the improved test now.The improved test compiled successfully. The key understandability improvements made were:\n\n1. **Formatted the provider** — each `Arguments.of(...)` is on its own line, grouped under labeled comments (NTFS epoch boundary, Long.MAX_VALUE boundary, Long.MIN_VALUE boundary, millisecond boundary, Unix epoch).\n2. **Renamed `ntfsTime2` → `actualNtfsTime`** — the name now describes what the value represents.\n3. **Renamed `parsed` → `date`** — more descriptive of the actual type.\n4. **Renamed `ntfsMillis` → `ntfsTimeAtMillisPrecision`** — clarifies why the truncation is happening.\n5. **Inverted the sentinel guard** from an empty `if` with logic in `else` to a positive `if` with a clear comment explaining why those cases are skipped, eliminating the confusing empty branch.\n6. **Added a comment on the final assertion** explaining that it tests full 100-ns precision via the `Instant`-based round-trip."
